      "text": "To find the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\), we first need to determine the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) after the translation of segment \\( s_1 \\).\n\nThe endpoints of segment \\( s_1 \\) are \\((4, 1)\\) and \\((-8, 5)\\).\n\nThe translation is given as 2 units to the right and 3 units up. This means we add 2 to the x-coordinates and 3 to the y-coordinates of each endpoint.\n\nFor the first endpoint \\((4, 1)\\):\n\\[\n(4 + 2, 1 + 3) = (6, 4)\n\\]\n\nFor the second endpoint \\((-8, 5)\\):\n\\[\n(-8 + 2, 5 + 3) = (-6, 8)\n\\]\n\nSo, the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\) are \\((6, 4)\\) and \\((-6, 8)\\).\n\nThe midpoint of a segment with endpoints \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the endpoints of segment \\( s_2 \\):\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{6 + (-6)}{2}, \\frac{4 + 8}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{0}{2}, \\frac{12}{2} \\right) = (0, 6)\n\\]\n\nThus, the midpoint of segment \\( s_2 \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0, 6)}\\).",
